'''ancient<sup>1</sup>''' (ān′shent), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''antient'' (a spelling but recently obsolete, after ''patient'', etc., or with ref. to the orig. L.), < ME. ''auncient'', ''auncyent'', ''aunciant'', etc. (with excrescent ''-t'', as in ''tyrant'', etc.: see ''-ant<sup>2</sup>''), earlier ''auncien'', ''auncian'', < OF. ''ancien'', mod. F. ''ancien'' = Pr. ''ancian'' = Sp. ''anciano'' = Pg. ''ancião'' = It. ''anziano'', < ML. ''antianus'', ''ancianus'', former, old, ancient, prop. *''anteanus'', with term. ''-anus'' (E. ''-an'', ''-en''), < L. ''ante'', before, whence also ''antīcus'', ''antīquus'', former, ancient, antique: see ''antic'', ''antique'', and ''ante-''.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Existent or occurring in time long past, usually in remote ages; belonging to or associated with antiquity; old, as opposed to ''modern'': as, ''ancient'' authors; ''ancient'' records. {{smaller|As specifically applied to history, ''ancient'' usually refers to times and events prior to the downfall of the Western Roman Empire, {{sc|a. d.}} 476, and is opposed to ''medieval'', which is applied to the period from about the fifth century to the end of the fifteenth, when modern history begins, and to ''modern'', which is sometimes used of the whole period since the fifth century. In other uses it commonly has no exact reference to time.}}
{{smaller block|We lost a great number of ''ancient'' authors by the conquest of Egypt by the Saracens, which deprived Europe of the use of the papyrus.
{{right|''I. D’Israeli'', Curios. of Lit., I. 67.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The voice I hear this passing night was heard
In ''ancient'' days by emperor and clown.</poem>
{{right|''Keats'', Ode to Nightingale.}}}}
{{smaller block|His [Milton’s] language even has caught the accent of the ''ancient'' world. {{right|''Lowell'', New Princeton Rev., I. 154.}}}}
'''2.''' Having lasted from a remote period; hav-

