'''II.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' One who lived in former ages; a person belonging to an early period of the world’s history: generally used in the plural.
{{smaller block|We meet with more raillery among the moderns, but more good sense among the ''ancients.''
{{right|''Addison'', Spectator, No. 249.}}}}
'''2.''' A very old man; hence, an elder or person of influence; a governor or ruler, political or ecclesiastical.
{{smaller block|Long since that white-haired ''ancient'' slept.
{{right|''Bryant'', Old Man’s Counsel.}}
The Lord will enter into judgment with the ''ancients'' of his people.
{{right|Is. iii. 14.}}}}
'''3†.''' A senior.
{{smaller block|In Christianity they were his ''ancients.'' {{right|''Hooker.''}}}}
'''4.''' In the Inns of Court and Chancery in London, one who has a certain standing or seniority: thus, in Gray’s Inn, the society consists of benchers, ''ancients'', barristers, and students under the bar, the ancients being the oldest barristers. ''Wharton.''
{{smaller block|When he was ''Auncient'' in Inne of Courte, certaine yong Ientlemen were brought before him, to be corrected for certaine misorders.
{{right|''Ascham'', The Scholemaster, p. 62.}}}}
'''Ancient of days''', the Supreme Being, in reference to his existence from eternity.

